{
  "id": "ps_practical",
  "title": "Pashto — practical English romanization (Afghan style)",
  "language": "ps",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "practical",
  "status": "informal",
  "ascii": true,
  "authority": "Afghan and Pakistani Pashtun usage (BGN/PCGN without diacritics); not a formal standard",
  "description": "Plain-ASCII spellings of Pashto names: retroflex letters as t d r n, ښ = sh, ږ = zh, څ = ts, ځ = dz, و = w, ې = e, final ی = ay (Zalmay), ۍ = ai (Malalai), final silent h = a (Malala), -ullah and -uddin joined (Hafizullah, Shamsuddin), Abdul + name. Known names come from the lexicon (latin['ps'], then Persian and Urdu forms).",
  "sources": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghan_name",
    "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5ab4e57ee5274a1aa5933455/ROMANIZATION_OF_PASHTO.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulbuddin_Hekmatyar"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Dialect splits are not modelled: ښ is sh (southern, Kandahar) not kh (northern, Peshawar: Pakhto), ږ is zh not g (Spozhmay/Spogmay).",
    "The Arabic article inside a compound is joined (Shamsuddin, Ziaulhaq); Abd + article gives Abdul + separate name (Abdul Rashid).",
    "Lexicon spellings win (Mohammad, Ahmad Shah, Hekmatyar); the rules only handle vocalised input and unknown names."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤئابپتټثجچځڅحخدډذرړزژږسشښصضطظعغفقکګگلمنڼوهیيېۍ",
